WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:01.935 align:start size:91% position:9%
(host)
Majestic, mysterious forms

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:03.403 align:start size:81% position:19%
that hold the secrets
of the universe,

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:07.106
or big, inscrutable rocks that
take a lot of effort to climb.

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:08.908
In pretty much every culture,

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.077 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's all about majesty
and mystery

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.012
when it comes to mountains.

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:15.248 align:start size:84% position:16%
Home to mighty deities,
sacred grounds,

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:17.050
and sublime phenomena,

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.453 align:start size:97% position:3%
mountains loom large
in our collective imagination.

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.689
<i> [intro music]</i>

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:26.825 align:start size:91% position:9%
Throughout history,
real and mythical mountains

00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:28.127
have been used to discuss

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:30.897 align:start size:91% position:9%
both natural
and supernatural activity,

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:33.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
from weather
and volcanic eruptions

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.102
to everlasting youth
and ethereal beings.

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:37.603
But they also embody

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:40.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
some of our most existential
questions about life--

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:43.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
our position in the universe,
our spiritual doubts,

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:46.045 align:start size:84% position:16%
and our desire
to transcend the self.

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:48.481 align:start position:0% line:0%
In the earliest written records
of human history,

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.050 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
mountains were depicted
as sources of life.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.885 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
Dating the
third millennium BCE,

00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:56.355 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
tablets found at the ancient
Sumerian city of Nippur,

00:00:56.355 --> 00:01:00.125 line:0%
now Iraq, are very clear about
where the first Sumerian god

00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:02.027 line:0%
spawned the rest of his team--

00:01:02.027 --> 00:01:03.996 align:start size:81% position:19%
"On the mountain
of heaven and earth,

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:06.166
Anu spawned the Anunnaki gods."

00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:08.401 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
"Hursag,"
meaning mountain or hill,

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.369 line:0%
is the first word
on the tablet,

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:14.273 line:0%
making it one of the oldest
surviving written words.

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:16.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
On the peak of the mountain,
the Anunnaki gods

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:18.377
created sheep and grain

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:21.347 align:start position:0%
before sending them down
to mortals living on the slopes,

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:24.450 align:start size:91% position:9%
thus allowing human society
to flourish.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.153 align:start size:88% position:13%
This ancient myth carries
a few key ideas

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.155 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are common
to mountain mythology.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:33.259
First, the mountain acts
as the site of creation.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.928 align:start size:91% position:9%
We see this idea unfolding
across the world.

00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:37.430 align:start size:75% position:25%
In ancient Tibet,
for instance,

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
the first ruler was believed
to be a god

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.335
who descended from the sky
using a rope of woven light

00:01:43.335 --> 00:01:45.505 align:start size:75% position:25%
to the peak
of Yarlha Shampo,

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:49.141
a place that would also serve
as the instrument of creation.

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:50.510
In the Hindu Puranas,

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.112 align:start size:84% position:16%
deities and demons tied
an enormous serpent

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:55.381 align:start size:78% position:22%
around the mythical
Mount Mandara,

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:57.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
then used the mountain
as a stick

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.785 align:start size:81% position:19%
for churning
the celestial ocean,

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:01.820
creating the universe itself.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:06.392
Second, the mountains are also
the dwelling place of the gods.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:09.428 align:start position:0%
Many cultures locate
their gods on a mountain summit.

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:13.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
The supreme Hindu deity Shiva
resides on Mount Kailash,

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:16.069 align:start size:88% position:13%
while in Greek mythology,
the principal gods

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:18.137
camp out on Mount Olympus.

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:20.573 align:start size:91% position:9%
These lofty abodes testify
to the awesome,

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:24.544 align:start position:0%
and frankly, sometimes
terrifying power of the divine.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:27.046 align:start size:88% position:13%
In Homer's<i> Iliad,</i>
Zeus boasts of his power

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:29.482 align:start size:88% position:13%
to drag the Earth and sea
up the mountain

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:33.552 align:start position:0%
on a golden rope and dangle them
off the sacred peak.

00:02:33.552 --> 00:02:36.456 line:0%
The third important feature
of the Sumerian creation myth

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:38.991 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
is a vision of mountains
of a life-giving,

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:40.593 line:0%
ever-present force

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:44.029 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
that provides both spiritual
and material sustenance.

00:02:44.029 --> 00:02:45.564 line:0%
In another Sumerian legend,

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:48.734 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
this belief is personified
in Ninhursag,

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:50.236 line:0%
goddess of the mountains,

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.972 align:start position:0% line:0%
who produced milk
and mothered humans and animals

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.707 line:0%
from her seat at the summit.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.041
And in Tibetan mythology,

00:02:56.041 --> 00:02:58.044 align:start size:75% position:25%
the Five Sisters
of Long Life

00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:00.480 align:start size:81% position:19%
each occupy their own
Himalayan perch.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.616 align:start size:97% position:3%
The resident goddess of
Mount Everest, Miyolangsangma,

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:07.152
is believed to be in charge
of inexhaustible giving.

00:03:07.152 --> 00:03:09.388 align:start size:81% position:19%
This fearsome goddess
rides a tiger,

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:11.224
a bowl of food in one hand,

00:03:11.224 --> 00:03:14.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
and a jewel-spitting mongoose
in the other.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:17.563 align:start size:97% position:3%
This illustrates her dual role
as both a supreme nurturer

00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:19.933
and a potentially
hazardous force,

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.701 align:start size:81% position:19%
rather like
the mountain itself.

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:25.505
As a mythical origin point
and lofty spiritual home

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:28.674
but also an enduring source
of food, land, and shelter

00:03:28.674 --> 00:03:31.511 align:start size:88% position:13%
for those who live below,
mountains are places

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:34.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the celestial
and terrestrial collide

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:36.316
with awe-inspiring results.

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:38.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
Throughout history,
mountains were not only used

00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:41.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
to explain the origins
and governing of the universe

00:03:41.753 --> 00:03:44.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
but were seen as forces
of cosmic balance

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:46.392
in their own right.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:49.428 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Mythical mountains
often function as<i> axis mundi,</i>

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:52.565 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
or a "stabilizing axis
that extends through the Earth

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.134 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
"to connect its surface
with the underworld

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:56.436 line:0%
and the heavens."

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:57.904
In Norse mythology,

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.239 align:start size:94% position:6%
a heavenly mountain was said
to rise at the point

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:02.508 align:start size:88% position:13%
where the rainbow
meets the dome of heaven,

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:05.778
thus acting as a force
for celestial harmony.

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.313
And in Iranian mythology,

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:10.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
the legendary Hara Berezaiti
was an enormous mountain

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:12.218 align:start size:84% position:16%
rising from the center
of the Earth

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:13.753
and tethered to the sky.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:16.288
The stars and moon
revolved around it,

00:04:16.288 --> 00:04:19.291 align:start size:88% position:13%
while the sun rested
behind its peak at night.

00:04:19.291 --> 00:04:21.560 align:start size:97% position:3%
In addition
to its spiritual significance,

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.163 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hara was used
in early scientific theories

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.498
of the universe.

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:27.599
Many other cultures
endorsed this idea

00:04:27.599 --> 00:04:30.135
of the mythical mountain
connecting the underground,

00:04:30.135 --> 00:04:31.771
Earthly, and heavenly planes.

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:35.308 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
Another axis of the world,
Mount Meru, or Sumeru,

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:38.744 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
is venerated in Hindu,
Jain, and Buddhist cultures.

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:42.247 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Hindu cosmology traces
specific mythical geographies

00:04:42.247 --> 00:04:44.349 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
around Meru,
with the mountain

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:46.552 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
occupying the center
of the universe,

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:48.887 line:0%
the city of the moon
rising in the north,

00:04:48.887 --> 00:04:51.991 align:start size:88% position:13%
the city of the ocean god
occupying the west,

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:54.894 align:start size:88% position:13%
the Devas, or divinities,
dwelling in the east,

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:58.263 align:start position:0%
the realms of Yama,
the god of death, in the south,

00:04:58.263 --> 00:05:00.199 align:start size:78% position:22%
and the Asanas,
or infernal demons,

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:01.734
lurking below.

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:05.471 align:start position:0% line:0%
Mount Meru was also used as
an astronomical reference point

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:08.508 align:start size:91% position:9%
with the pole star position
directly above its peak

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:12.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
tied to other heavenly bodies
by ropes of wind.

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:14.247
In ancient Jainian philosophy,

00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:16.482 align:start size:88% position:13%
Meru was also used
to theorize the movement

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.417
of celestial bodies.

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:20.452
As these axis mundi show,

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:22.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
mountains were at the center
of human attempts

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:25.657 align:start position:0%
to explain the cosmic structure
of the universe

00:05:25.657 --> 00:05:28.894
as well as the spiritual
significance of its realms.

00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:30.196
Beyond the natural world,

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:32.598 align:start position:0%
mountains were referenced
throughout ancient architecture

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:35.635 align:start size:84% position:16%
that evoked the sacred
and aspirational.

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:37.703 align:start size:88% position:13%
Both Hindu
and Buddhist architecture

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:40.373 align:start size:88% position:13%
reinforces the importance
of Mount Meru

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:43.475
with towers and temples
evoking its sacred peak

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:45.744
and garden monuments
or water structures

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:48.513 align:start size:81% position:19%
echoing its position
in the universe.

00:05:48.513 --> 00:05:50.983 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
In ancient Mesopotamia,
raised structures

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:54.921 align:start position:0% line:0%
called<i> ziggurats</i> were built
as dwelling places for deities.

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:57.256 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
These consisted
of up to seven stories

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.592 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
that echoed
ascending heavenly tiers

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:02.494 line:0%
with only priests permitted
to enter their base layers

00:06:02.494 --> 00:06:05.264 line:0%
and the upper layers
reserved for the gods.

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:06.565 line:0%
And in ancient Egypt,

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:08.801 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
the primordial mound
known as Benben

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:11.471 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
was revered for its status
as the first item

00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:13.438 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
that emerged
from a watery abyss

00:06:13.438 --> 00:06:14.974 line:0%
at the beginning of the world.

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.810 line:0%
This sacred mound provided
a template for the capstones

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.645 line:0%
of the pyramids
and surfaces

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:22.315 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
throughout Egyptian symbols
and iconography.

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.684 align:start size:88% position:13%
In other cultures,
the worship of mountains

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:26.952 align:start size:84% position:16%
blends natural
and mythical phenomena.

00:06:26.952 --> 00:06:29.755 align:start size:88% position:13%
In Hindu cosmology,
Mount Kailash is not only

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:32.357 align:start size:91% position:9%
the home
of the powerful deity Shiva

00:06:32.357 --> 00:06:34.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the source
of the holy river Ganges,

00:06:34.693 --> 00:06:37.796
which cascades from heaven
and pours through his hair

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:39.899
before continuing on
throughout the land.

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.067 align:start size:78% position:22%
Here, the mountain
is seen again

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:45.304
as a life-giving force
with awesome powers.

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:47.639 align:start size:84% position:16%
In the Diné,
or Navajo origin story,

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:49.642 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Creator
placed the first people

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:51.644 align:start size:78% position:22%
in the middle
of four mountains--

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:53.346 line:92%
Blanca Peak, Mount Taylor,

00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:54.580 line:92%
San Francisco Peak,

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.315 line:92%
and Hesperus Mountain.

00:06:56.315 --> 00:06:58.984 line:91%
Here, the mountains
delineate a homeland,

00:06:58.984 --> 00:07:01.853 align:start size:84% position:16% line:91%
one that once stretched
over a vast space

00:07:01.853 --> 00:07:04.690 align:start size:88% position:13% line:91%
but was protected by
these natural fortresses.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.425 align:start size:81% position:19%
Elsewhere, the power
of the mountain

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:08.327
is a little more ambivalent.

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:09.661
In Hawaiian mythology,

00:07:09.661 --> 00:07:11.864 align:start size:81% position:19%
the goddess Pele
commanded volcanoes.

00:07:11.864 --> 00:07:14.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
She was believed to have
created the Hawaiian islands

00:07:14.833 --> 00:07:17.870 align:start size:97% position:3%
and was just as feared for
her ability to cause eruptions

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.973 align:start size:97% position:3%
as she was revered for
the creation of rich new land.

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:23.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Aztecs also
honored volcanic mountains--

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:27.146 align:start size:97% position:3%
for example, in the form of
Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl.

00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:30.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
According to myth,
Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:32.718 align:start size:84% position:16%
were once young lovers
who were separated

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
when Popocatépetl
was called off to war.

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:37.190 align:start size:81% position:19%
Believing her beloved
had perished,

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:39.859 align:start size:88% position:13%
Iztaccíhuatl wasted away
from grief,

00:07:39.859 --> 00:07:43.329 align:start position:0%
only for Popocatépetl to return
very much alive.

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:46.999 align:start position:0%
Distraught, he constructed
a funeral mound for Iztaccíhuatl

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:48.601
and lay by her side,

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:51.904
with their bodies eventually
transforming into mountains.

00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:53.839 align:start size:84% position:16%
The rising smoke
was believed to signal

00:07:53.839 --> 00:07:56.075 align:start size:81% position:19%
either their eternal
funeral torches

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:57.743
or their still-burning love.

00:07:57.743 --> 00:08:00.779
While these examples venerate
and explain natural phenomena,

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:03.349 align:start size:91% position:9%
they also show
how people's understanding

00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:05.517 align:start size:81% position:19%
of mountains
as natural resources

00:08:05.517 --> 00:08:08.320 align:start size:91% position:9%
was always entwined
with ancient storytelling.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.323 align:start size:88% position:13%
While mythical mountains
have generated

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:12.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
their own cosmological,
astronomical,

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:14.327
and geological theories,

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:15.961 align:start size:78% position:22%
they're also
supernatural worlds

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:18.964 align:start size:97% position:3%
that no human knowledge system
can account for.

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:21.167 align:start size:81% position:19%
In Chinese mythology,
for instance,

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:23.702 align:start position:0%
Mount Kunlun is
the mountainous home of deities

00:08:23.702 --> 00:08:26.105 align:start size:81% position:19%
and immortals
that props up the sky

00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.707
and forms a ladder
from heaven to Earth.

00:08:28.707 --> 00:08:31.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
Guarded by a tiger
with nine human faces,

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:35.848
Kunlun is a space of abundance,
from the peaches of immortality

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:38.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
and grain that can grow
up to 40 feet

00:08:38.484 --> 00:08:40.819 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the self-regenerating
cow's meat.

00:08:40.819 --> 00:08:43.822 align:start size:91% position:9%
While these strange riches
are not easy to access,

00:08:43.822 --> 00:08:46.358 align:start size:81% position:19%
ancient texts
promise many rewards

00:08:46.358 --> 00:08:47.826
for climbing Mount Kunlun.

00:08:47.826 --> 00:08:50.629
Reaching the first tier
would bestow immortality,

00:08:50.629 --> 00:08:53.565
the second, the ability
to control wind and rain,

00:08:53.565 --> 00:08:56.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the third would grant you
permanent access

00:08:56.201 --> 00:08:57.669
to the divine realm--

00:08:57.669 --> 00:08:59.705
not bad a reward
after a big hike.

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:02.841 align:start position:0%
To the east of Kunlun
is another mountainous paradise,

00:09:02.841 --> 00:09:05.510 align:start size:91% position:9%
Mount Penglai, which is
governed by eight immortals

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:08.414 align:start size:88% position:13%
who can create
and destroy life at will.

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:10.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
In this land
of brilliant sunshine,

00:09:10.682 --> 00:09:13.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
immortality is guaranteed
for all.

00:09:13.485 --> 00:09:15.720
The water conveys
eternal youth,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.857
there is no knowledge
or sickness or pain,

00:09:18.857 --> 00:09:21.093 align:start size:78% position:22%
fruit remains ripe
all year round,

00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:23.729 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the bowls of rice
are perpetually brimming.

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:26.532
Referred to as<i> Horai</i>
in Japanese literature,

00:09:26.532 --> 00:09:28.767 align:start size:84% position:16%
one Shinto text
describes the mountain

00:09:28.767 --> 00:09:30.569
as not only a paradise,

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:33.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
but an alien space
that stretches the limits

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:34.773
of human perception.

00:09:34.773 --> 00:09:36.641
No matter your belief system,

00:09:36.641 --> 00:09:39.378 align:start size:81% position:19%
mountains are
indisputably sublime,

00:09:39.378 --> 00:09:40.679
which is why they remain

00:09:40.679 --> 00:09:43.015 align:start size:81% position:19%
near universal sites
of pilgrimage.

00:09:43.015 --> 00:09:46.419 align:start position:0%
As sites of spiritual excursion,
mountains promise

00:09:46.419 --> 00:09:49.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
a transcendence
of earthly conditions.

00:09:49.021 --> 00:09:51.790 align:start size:91% position:9%
From the barefoot journeys
undertaken by Catholics

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.359 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
to climb the rocky slopes
of Croagh Patrick--

00:09:54.359 --> 00:09:57.663 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
one of the highest and holiest
mountain in Ireland--

00:09:57.663 --> 00:10:00.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
to Hindu pilgrimages
to the sacred hill Govardhan,

00:10:00.599 --> 00:10:03.869 align:start position:0%
home to temples
and the site of divine activity,

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:06.305 align:start size:88% position:13%
devotees visit and climb
mountains as a ritual

00:10:06.305 --> 00:10:09.375
that signals spiritual
and literal ascension.

00:10:09.375 --> 00:10:13.011 align:start position:0%
While mountain climbing is
an ostensibly secular activity,

00:10:13.011 --> 00:10:16.348
many climbers have spoken
of a similar aspiration

00:10:16.348 --> 00:10:17.649
to transcend the self

00:10:17.649 --> 00:10:19.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
and reach higher plains
of understanding.

00:10:19.918 --> 00:10:22.821 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mountains, then, are not only
enormous landmarks;

00:10:22.821 --> 00:10:25.056 align:start size:94% position:6%
they inspire
some pretty strong feelings.

00:10:25.056 --> 00:10:28.260 align:start size:88% position:13%
As seeds of creation
and axes of the universe,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
they've helped people
narrate where they came from

00:10:30.929 --> 00:10:33.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
and anchored our sense
of location

00:10:33.232 --> 00:10:35.166
in an often-confusing world.

00:10:35.166 --> 00:10:38.170
But they're also sites
of magic and mystery

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:39.471
in their own right.

00:10:39.471 --> 00:10:42.207 align:start position:0%
Whether using mountains
to track the movements of stars,

00:10:42.207 --> 00:10:45.311
curry favor with the gods,
or explain seismic shifts,

00:10:45.311 --> 00:10:49.048
it's clear that mountains have
always driven our imaginations

00:10:49.048 --> 00:10:50.683
to new heights.

00:10:51.817 --> 00:10:53.485 align:start size:78% position:22%
History lovers,
we need to tell you

00:10:53.485 --> 00:10:57.022
about a new series from PBS
called "The Bigger Picture."

00:10:57.022 --> 00:10:58.857 align:start size:88% position:13%
Hosted by
Professor Vincent Brown,

00:10:58.857 --> 00:11:01.260 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's a show that examines
famous photographs

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:04.362 align:start position:0%
and unpacks the
historical context around them.

00:11:04.362 --> 00:11:07.166 align:start size:94% position:6%
In doing so, we reveal
what these photos can tell us

00:11:07.166 --> 00:11:10.135 align:start size:91% position:9%
about our history, but also
how we view ourselves.

00:11:10.135 --> 00:11:12.771 align:start size:97% position:3%
Check out the first episode
at the link in the description

00:11:12.771 --> 00:11:14.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
and head to
the PBS YouTube channel

00:11:14.607 --> 00:11:15.841
to see it yourself.

00:11:15.841 --> 00:11:18.044 align:start size:88% position:13%
Tell them "Fate &amp; Fabled"
sent you.

00:11:20.212 --> 00:11:23.215 line:0%
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

